From a7b22052b33c4741b912dd8f6f0d0588bc947444 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yungtry <yungtry@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 21:58:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Ptrace: hide FEX's own SIGBUS alignment stops from the tracer

On arm64 an unaligned atomic or exclusive access in JIT code raises a
host SIGBUS with si_code BUS_ADRALN. FEX's SigbusHandler emulates the
access and resumes; the guest never sees it. In a traced process the
host kernel reports every such SIGBUS to the tracer as a
signal-delivery stop before FEX's handler runs. FixupWaitStatus already
hides FEX's own SIGTRAP, SIGSEGV and SIGPROF stops. SIGBUS was missing.

The Proton Easy Anti-Cheat launcher (bootstrapper 1.9.3, The Division 2)
injects its client into the game process it starts under ptrace. While
the client initialises it makes many unaligned atomic accesses. Its
tracer saw one SIGBUS stop for each, 0x77f in wait4 (1000 in one
launch), and passed each back with PTRACE_CONT. It then gave up:
"Launcher finished with: 210, 'Unexpected error. (#2) (0)'" about 0.8 s
after "Starting Wine module mapping", and EACLaunch.exe crashed on its
error path.

When a FEX tracee stops with SIGBUS, read its siginfo. If si_code is
BUS_ADRALN, re-inject the signal with PTRACE_CONT and keep waiting, as
for the other internal stops. x86-64 user code has no BUS_ADRALN fault
(alignment checking with EFLAGS.AC needs CR0.AM, which Linux leaves
off for user space). Every other SIGBUS, such as BUS_ADRERR from a
mapping past the end of its file, is the guest's and stays visible.

Result: the launcher ends with "301, 'Easy Anti-Cheat successfully
loaded in-game'" and The Division 2 loads into the world.

Limits: only wait4 is filtered, as before; waitid is still passed
through.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/PtraceEmu.cpp     | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/PtraceEmu.cpp b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/PtraceEmu.cpp
index 83efef3..99b4e4b 100644
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/PtraceEmu.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/PtraceEmu.cpp
@@ -672,6 +672,20 @@ bool FixupWaitStatus(pid_t Pid, int* Status) {
     return true;
   }
 
+  // On arm64 FEX also takes a host SIGBUS (BUS_ADRALN) for an unaligned atomic or exclusive access in JIT code and
+  // emulates the access in its handler (SigbusHandler in SignalDelegator.cpp); the guest never sees it. x86-64 user code
+  // has no such fault, so a BUS_ADRALN stop of a FEX tracee is always FEX's own. Code that does many such accesses (the
+  // Easy Anti-Cheat client while it initialises in the game process) otherwise shows its tracer a stream of SIGBUS
+  // stops; the launcher passes each one back and gives up after 1000 with "Unexpected error. (#2)". Any other SIGBUS
+  // (BUS_ADRERR from a mapping past the end of its file, ...) is the guest's and stays visible.
+  if ((St >> 16) == 0 && WSTOPSIG(St) == SIGBUS && IsFexTracee(Pid)) {
+    siginfo_t Info {};
+    if (HostPtrace(PTRACE_GETSIGINFO, Pid, nullptr, &Info) == 0 && Info.si_code == BUS_ADRALN) {
+      HostPtrace(PTRACE_CONT, Pid, nullptr, reinterpret_cast<void*>(static_cast<uintptr_t>(SIGBUS)));
+      return true;
+    }
+  }
+
   if ((St >> 16) == 0 && WSTOPSIG(St) == SIGPROF && IsFexTracee(Pid)) {
     // FEX's own sampling profiler signal: never visible to the guest tracer.
     HostPtrace(PTRACE_CONT, Pid, nullptr, nullptr);
